### Architectural Observation

I think this phase introduces what may become one of the most important concepts in the entire COREI architecture.

Today, almost every frontend framework says:

Build components.

COREI should instead say:

Build capabilities.

A capability may internally contain:

20 React components
AG Grid
TradingView
React Flow
Monaco
xterm.js
Plotly
AI services
Backend APIs
Streaming data
Commands
Context

Yet, to the Workbench, it is simply one Operational Capability.

For example:

Market Investigation Capability
│
├── Chart Surface
├── Order Flow Surface
├── Market Depth Surface
├── Liquidity Surface
├── News Surface
├── AI Analysis Surface
├── Command Integration
├── Search Integration
├── Notification Integration
├── Context Integration
└── Workbench Contracts

Notice that React completely disappears from the architecture.

This is exactly where we want to be.

The engineer may think about React.

The architecture thinks about Institutional Capabilities.

The operator thinks about professional work.

That separation of concerns is, in my view, one of the strongest architectural decisions we've made throughout Stage-23 and is fully consistent with the COREI Anchor Law, the Master Trader Persona, and the long-term vision of COREI as an institutional operating system rather than a collection of web components.

