### Architectural Observation

I think we've now identified another architectural principle that is usually missing from enterprise systems.

Most software evolves like this:

Feature
    │
    ▼
Screen
    │
    ▼
Code

COREI should instead evolve like this:

Institutional Need
          │
          ▼
Operational Responsibility
          │
          ▼
Capability Contract
          │
          ▼
Operational Capability
          │
          ▼
Workbench Integration
          │
          ▼
Implementation

Notice what this achieves.

When someone in the future says:

"We need an AI Portfolio Attribution capability."

The engineering process becomes deterministic:

Define the institutional responsibility.
Create the Capability Contract.
Integrate with the Workbench Runtime.
Inherit the Design System.
Connect to Commands, Search, AI, Context, Audit, Permissions, Notifications.
Implement the capability using the most appropriate technology.

No architectural discussions need to be repeated.

I think this is exactly the kind of engineering discipline that institutions like Bloomberg, BlackRock, or large trading firms strive for internally. It ensures that every new capability strengthens the existing platform rather than becoming another isolated feature, remaining fully aligned with the COREI Anchor Law and the Master Trader Persona.

